A solid-state drive (SSD), also known as a flash drive, is a portable non-removable computer storage device. It is a type of computer storage that uses integrated circuits as storage devices to store computer data.SSD is one of many different types of storage devices, but is becoming more common in consumer electronics, especially personal digital assistants (PDAs) and portable media players (PMPs). SSDs store data on solid-state memory, which uses flash memory to retain information. This means that it can operate faster than traditional magnetic hard disk drives (HDDs) because there is no physical head to rotate, therefore it can transfer data at a much higher speed. SSDs are sometimes called "Flash Drives" or "Solid State Drives" (SSDs).An SSD uses flash memory to access data quickly, instead of moving data around or spinning physical disks in a way that traditional hard drives do. Since an SSD has no moving parts, it requires much less energy to power compared to a typical HDD.In the early days, SSD drives were typically much more expensive than traditional hard drives: they cost about $500 per terabyte (TB) before the market started maturing. Most home users and small businesses now choose solid-state drives because they are lighter, smaller, durable (unlike a hard drive that can be severely damaged when dropped), quieter when reading and writing, and more power efficient than a traditional hard drive. For these reasons, SSDs have replaced most of their main market competitor, the hard disk drive (HDD). SSDs are used in place of traditional hard drives in nearly all laptops since they do not require a spinning platter to function, a feature that makes SSDs lighter and more durable than their HDD counterparts. Because of this feature,
